</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>AWCP four-phase message sequence between Delegator and Executor.
Solid, dashed, and blue arrows denote synchronous HTTP requests, responses, and asynchronous SSE events, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AWCP formalizes the delegation lifecycle through two cooperating state machines, one located on each side of the protocol, which are synchronized by a four-phase message exchange.
Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Delegation Lifecycle ‣ 3 AWCP Framework ‣ AWCP: A Workspace Delegation Protocol for Deep-Engagement Collaboration across Remote Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the corresponding message sequence.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dual state machines.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The protocol separates delegation governance into two distinct state machines that operate independently yet synchronize at well-defined points through protocol messages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DelegationStateMachine</span> operates on the Delegator side and tracks nine states: an initial state <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">created</span>, four intermediate states (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">invited</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">accepted</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">started</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">running</span>), which represent progression through the negotiation, provisioning, and execution phases, and four terminal states (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">completed</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">error</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cancelled</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">expired</span>).
Transitions are triggered by nine event types, each governed by transition guards that restrict activation to specific source states.
The happy path follows the sequence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{created}\to\texttt{invited}\to\texttt{accepted}\to\texttt{started}\to\texttt{running}\to\texttt{completed}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">created</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">invited</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">accepted</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">started</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">running</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">completed</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{created}\to\texttt{invited}\to\texttt{accepted}\to\texttt{started}\to\texttt{running}\to\texttt{completed}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From any non-terminal state, an <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ERROR</span> or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CANCEL</span> event transitions the machine to the corresponding terminal state; an <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXPIRE</span> event is additionally valid from <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">invited</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">accepted</span>, or <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">running</span> when the lease duration elapses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AssignmentStateMachine</span> operates on the Executor side with a deliberately minimal four-state design: <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pending</span> (awaiting the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">START</span> message after accepting an invitation), <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">active</span> (task executing), <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">completed</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">error</span>.
This asymmetry reflects the protocol’s division of responsibility: the Delegator manages the full lifecycle including lease enforcement and snapshot reconciliation, while the Executor focuses exclusively on task execution within the provisioned workspace.
Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Dual state machines. ‣ 3.2 Delegation Lifecycle ‣ 3 AWCP Framework ‣ AWCP: A Workspace Delegation Protocol for Deep-Engagement Collaboration across Remote Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the formal state transition structure of both machines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure">
